   <h3> Loot Distribution</h3>
   <p>
   
<p>The rules and guidelines set forth here are to make item distribution fair 
  for everyone, including non-Bloodhorde members that may join us on raids, or 
  be apart of your hunting group.</p>
<p><b>Item types</b></p>
<p>For Bloodhorde item distibution rules and guidelines, there are 5 categories 
  of items that start at the most general, and progressively get more specific 
  (better):</p>
<ol>
  <li>Crap items - these are usually the &quot;common&quot; drops from MoBs that 
    nobody wants</li>
  <li> Tradable (not &quot;no drop&quot;) items</li>
  <li>No drop items</li>
  <li>Class specific items - items are considered &quot;class specific&quot; if 
    there are 2 or fewer classes that can use them (i.e. class specific planar 
    armor, or a monk/warrior only staff, etc.). You should not roll on class specific 
    items unless there is nobody of the class in question present. Only classes 
    that can use the item should be allowed to roll for them.</li>
  <li>Class specific breastplates or leg armor</li>
</ol>
<p>Once you have rolled and won an item from one category, you are not allowed 
  to roll for another item from that category until all other members of your 
  group/raiding party/class have already won something from that category also. 
</p>
<p>The system works like this:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Find the most specific category that the item falls under</li>
  <li>If you have not won anything from that category, you may roll on it.</li>
  <li>If you win the item, that category and any other categories that the item 
    falls into is now off limits to you until all other people present have also 
    won something from those categories.</li>
</ol>
<p>For example, we are in the Plane of Fear, and the Umbral Breastplate drops 
  (SK class, no drop). The breastplate falls under the most specific category, 
  #5 (class specific breastplates/legs). An SK at the raid has not won either 
  the breastplate or legs so he rolls and wins. Now, category #5 was just the 
  most specific for the BP, but the BP is also &quot;no drop&quot; and class specific, 
  so it falls under categories #3 (no drop), #4 (class specific), and #5. Therefore, 
  the SK that won the BP is now off limits to all items that fall in #3, #4, and 
  #5 until all other people that qualify for items in those categories have all 
  won something also.</p>
<p>Another example. There is a group of 6 people in lower guk camping the Magi 
  for the Shiny Metallic Robe. They kill the Magi and he drops his common item 
  &quot;Silver-silk leggings&quot;. Most people consider this his &quot;crap item&quot; 
  as most people camp the Magi for the SMR. It would be unfair to prevent somebody 
  from rolling on the SMR because they won the leggings. Hence the reason for 
  the &quot;crap&quot; category. In this case, the leggings would fall under the 
  #1 &quot;crap&quot; category and the person that wins them would still be able 
  to roll for the SMR if it were to drop. Note that it should be clearly stated 
  what items are considered to be &quot;crap&quot; as it may not be perfectly 
  clear. Some high level dungeons have common loot that could be considered &quot;crap&quot;, 
  but still maybe worth several thousand plat. The group leader should clearly 
  state what is considered &quot;crap&quot;, if anything.</p>
<p><b>Guideline for Long Group Camps</b></p>
<p>Often, a group may decide to camp a specific MoB, or section of a dungeon for 
  an extended period of time. During this time group members may leave and new 
  members may join. To make things more fair for the people that have been at 
  the camp longer, we recommend disallowing new group members from rolling on 
  the first two drops (unless everybody else in the group has already won an item) 
  after they have joined the group. Basically, this gives group members that have 
  been there longer a better chance at winning a good item, and prevents somebody 
  from joining for 10 minutes and winning the rare drop.</p>
<p>The recommendation of disallowing new group members from rolling on the first 
  two drops is just a guideline. If the spawn is really fast and drops things 
  often, maybe disallowing them to roll on the first 3 or 4 would be appropriate. 
  If the spawn is extremely slow, maybe disallowing them to roll only once or 
  just doing away with this guideline altogether would be appropriate.</p>
<p>This should not be used to overly deny somebody a chance at an item, but rather 
  should be used to make things fair for those who have been at the camp longest. 
  Again, this is just a guideline. If you and your group can come up with a better 
  plan that better suits your situation, go for it!</p>
<p><b>Alternate Characters</b></p>
<p>This is somewhat &quot;honor system&quot; based. All people should know that 
  their alternate characters come after other people's primary character. If you 
  have an alternate character and an item that your alt can use drops, you should 
  allow other primary characters to roll first. Be honest, be honorable.</p>
<p><b>Honor or Need Override</b></p>
<p>In rare cases, Bloodhorde leaders may choose to override standard item and 
  loot distribution procedures and award an item to a member based on honor or 
  need. In cases like this, it should be noted that Bloodhorde leadership will 
  attempt to be as fair as possible. If you have a problem with an override decision, 
  you should discuss it with a Circle of Bones in private.</p>

